.TE
.PP
Clicks map through \f[CR]window_screen_to_buffer()\f[R] (core,
\f[CR]terminal.c\f[R]), which inverts the renderer\[cq]s visual\-row
walk \[em] line\-number gutter, collapsed folds, horizontal scroll,
soft\-wrap sublines and block\-below virtual text rows are all accounted
for.
Clicks on a virtual\-text row snap to its anchor line; clicks past
end\-of\-line clamp to the last character; clicks below EOF clamp to the
last line.
.PP
A selection made by dragging survives button release (vim\-like); the
next click or any mode change collapses it.
Releases, middle and right buttons are otherwise ignored.
.PP
Mouse events bypass keymap dispatch, macro recording and multicursor key
replay entirely, so they work identically in the vim, emacs and vscode
keymaps and can\[cq]t corrupt a recorded macro.
.SH How it works
Core enables xterm button\-event tracking (DEC 1002) with SGR encoding
(DEC 1006) while raw mode is active, parses
\f[CR]ESC [ < b ; x ; y M/m\f[R] into a \f[CR]MouseEvent\f[R]
(\f[CR]src/input/input.h\f[R]) and fires \f[CR]HOOK_MOUSE\f[R].
This plugin owns all semantics \[em] disable it (or
\f[CR]:mouse off\f[R]) and the editor is fully keyboard\-driven again.
.PP
Reporting is re\-armed after every shell\-out (\f[CR]:fmt\f[R],
\f[CR]:git\f[R], fzf, tmux) because \f[CR]enable_raw_mode()\f[R] carries
the desired state.
.SH Caveats
.IP \[bu] 2
While reporting is on, the terminal\[cq]s own selection is unavailable;
most terminals offer Shift+drag as a bypass, or use
\f[CR]:mouse off\f[R].
.IP \[bu] 2
Inside tmux, events reach hed as long as the inner program requests them
\[em] but \f[CR]set \-g mouse on\f[R] makes tmux consume them itself.
.IP \[bu] 2
Modal windows and active prompts ignore the mouse.
.IP \[bu] 2
Wheel\-scrolling drags the cursor along with the view edge, since the
renderer re\-snaps the viewport to the cursor every frame.
